{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current DailyMed label.","fda_source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d%22&limit=5"],"proposed_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current FDA/openFDA label.","rationale":"The no-boxed-warning conclusion is supportable only with an FDA source. DailyMed is not permissible for this field under the audit rules.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"rufinamide","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"rufinamide","rationale":"FDA labeling, FDA orphan-drug records, PubMed RCT records, and EMA Inovelon EPAR all identify the active substance/generic as rufinamide.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=193504","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/inovelon"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"FDA records show Banzel/rufinamide marketing approval on 11/14/2008 and FDA labeling identifies U.S. approval in 2008.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=193504","https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Available in US","field":"filter_availability","proposed_value":"Available in US","rationale":"Matches FDA-approved U.S. product status.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=193504"]},{"current_value":"LGS","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"LGS","rationale":"FDA and EMA labeling support adjunctive treatment of seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. Note that several included efficacy RCTs are partial-onset seizure studies, so the efficacy plot evidence is broader than the labeled LGS indication.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/inovelon"]},{"current_value":"LGS","field":"filter_epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"LGS","rationale":"Supported by FDA indication and EMA EPAR.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/inovelon"]},{"current_value":"Current ASM for seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.","field":"status_or_notes","proposed_value":"Current ASM for seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.","rationale":"FDA and EMA sources identify rufinamide as add-on/adjunctive therapy for seizures associated with LGS.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/inovelon"]},{"current_value":"Banzel; Inovelon","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Banzel; Inovelon","rationale":"Banzel is the FDA-listed U.S. brand; EMA identifies Inovelon as rufinamide. These are aliases/trade names for the same drug, not separate generic rows.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=193504","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/inovelon"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No alternate generic name was confirmed from trusted sources; Banzel and Inovelon are trade names and CGP 33101 is a research/code alias rather than an alternate generic name.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/inovelon"]},{"current_value":"CGP 33101","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"CGP 33101","rationale":"The alias is plausible, but the row's named sources do not document it and this audit did not confirm it in an allowed trusted source page.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":[]},{"current_value":"Tablet; oral suspension","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Tablet; oral suspension","rationale":"FDA labeling lists film-coated tablets and oral suspension.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Liquid; Tablet","field":"filter_formulation","proposed_value":"Liquid; Tablet","rationale":"Matches FDA-labeled oral suspension and tablets.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"3200 mg/day adult max","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"3200 mg/day adult max","rationale":"FDA adult dosing titrates to a maximum daily dose of 3200 mg/day in two divided doses.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"400 mg/day adult starting dose; titrate to 3200 mg/day target/max","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Adults: start 400-800 mg/day in two divided doses and titrate by 400-800 mg every other day to 3200 mg/day maximum; Elger2010 dose-ranging partial-seizure study reported a minimally efficacious dose of 400 mg/day.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports 400-800 mg/day as the adult starting dose and 3200 mg/day as the adult target/max. The 'minimum effective' wording is better supported by the Elger Phase II dose-ranging RCT for partial seizures, not by the labeled LGS adult dose statement alone.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20061123/"]},{"current_value":"LGS: adults 400-3200 mg/day divided BID; pediatric weight-based","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"LGS: adults start 400-800 mg/day divided BID and titrate to 3200 mg/day; pediatric patients start 10 mg/kg/day divided BID and titrate to 45 mg/kg/day, not to exceed 3200 mg/day.","rationale":"Current text is directionally correct but less precise than FDA dosing language.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"6-10 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"6-10 h","rationale":"FDA clinical pharmacology states plasma half-life is approximately 6-10 hours.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Carboxylesterase hydrolysis; renal excretion of metabolites","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Carboxylesterase-mediated hydrolysis to inactive metabolite CGP 47292; renal excretion mainly as metabolites, with less than 2% unchanged drug in urine.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports carboxylesterase-mediated hydrolysis, no CYP450-dependent metabolism, and minimal unchanged urinary recovery.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Renal/no major metabolism","field":"filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Carboxylesterase hydrolysis/no major CYP metabolism","rationale":"FDA labeling states most elimination is via metabolism and that the primary pathway is carboxylesterase-mediated hydrolysis; therefore 'Renal/no major metabolism' is too broad and partly contradicted.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Weak CYP3A4 inducer; not a major inhibitor","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Weak CYP3A4 inducer; little or no inhibition of most CYP enzymes at clinically relevant concentrations, with weak CYP2E1 inhibition noted in vitro.","rationale":"FDA labeling identifies rufinamide as a weak CYP3A4 inducer and little/no inhibitor of most CYP enzymes, with weak CYP2E1 inhibition in vitro.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Inhibitor","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"Inducer","rationale":"The filter conflicts with the row fact and FDA label; the clinically relevant direction is weak CYP3A4 induction, not inhibitor.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Precise mechanism is unknown; in vitro studies suggest sodium-channel modulation, especially prolongation of the inactive state and limitation of repetitive sodium-dependent firing.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Precise mechanism is unknown; in vitro studies suggest sodium-channel modulation, especially prolongation of the inactive state and limitation of repetitive sodium-dependent firing.","rationale":"FDA label supports this mechanism language.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Sodium channel","field":"filter_mechanism","proposed_value":"Sodium channel","rationale":"FDA label supports sodium-channel modulation as the principal in vitro mechanism.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Moderate","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Moderate","rationale":"The confidence label is consistent with FDA wording that the precise mechanism is unknown but in vitro sodium-channel evidence exists.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA Banzel prescribing information","rationale":"The mechanism is supported by FDA labeling; DailyMed should not be named as the source when trusted source policy can be satisfied directly with FDA labeling.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"FDA label","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"FDA label","rationale":"Correct tier for the mechanism source.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Shortens QT interval; caution with familial short QT syndrome or other QT-shortening drugs","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"Shortens QT interval; contraindicated in familial short QT syndrome and use caution with other QT-shortening drugs.","rationale":"FDA label states QT shortening, contraindication in familial short QT syndrome, and caution with other QT-shortening drugs.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"QT shortening","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"QT shortening","rationale":"Supported by FDA label.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"CNS: somnolence 24%, GI: vomiting 22%, CNS: headache 16%, CNS: dizziness 14%, GI: nausea 12%, dermatologic: rash 5%, cardiac: QT shortening 14%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: somnolence 24.3% in LGS RCT; GI: vomiting 21.6% in LGS RCT; CNS: headache 16% pediatric pooled / 27% adult pooled; CNS: dizziness 8% pediatric pooled / 19% adult pooled; GI: nausea 7% pediatric pooled / 12% adult pooled; dermatologic: rash 4% pediatric pooled; cardiac: QT shortening >20 msec at Tmax in 46% at 3200 mg vs 5-10% placebo in QT study.","rationale":"Somnolence 24% and vomiting 22% are supported by Glauser2008/FDA LGS trial data. Headache 16%, nausea 12%, and rash near 4% are supported in FDA pooled tables, but dizziness 14% and QT shortening 14% were not supported; FDA label gives dizziness 8% pediatric or 19% adult and QT shortening >20 msec in 46% at 3200 mg.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/","https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"CNS; Cardiac; Dermatologic; GI","field":"filter_symptom_category","proposed_value":"CNS; Cardiac; Dermatologic; GI","rationale":"The retained adverse-event categories are supported, although specific percentages need correction.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA Banzel prescribing information; FDA/openFDA drug label API; FDA Orphan Drug Designations and Approvals; NCBI Bookshelf LiverTox rufinamide monograph; NCBI Bookshelf StatPearls rufinamide; EMA Inovelon EPAR; PubMed RCT records.","rationale":"The named LiverTox anticonvulsants table is not a good support for LGS-specific row facts, Epilepsy Foundation Australia is outside the requested trusted-source domains, and DailyMed should not be used for FDA boxed-warning verification. FDA, NCBI, EMA, and PubMed sources support the retained facts.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=193504","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548457/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557595/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/inovelon"]},{"current_value":"2008","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"2008","rationale":"FDA labeling states Initial U.S. Approval: 2008, and FDA orphan records list marketing approval on 11/14/2008.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=193504"]}],"generic_name":"rufinamide","missing_rcts":[],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"9.6-23 % (drug minus placebo RR50 differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Biton2010 rufinamide 3200 mg/day 18.2%; Brodie2009 rufinamide 3200 mg/day 9.6%; Palhagen2001 rufinamide up to 1600 mg/day 23%)","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"9.6-23 % (drug minus placebo RR50 differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Biton2010 rufinamide 3200 mg/day 18.2%; Brodie2009 rufinamide 3200 mg/day 9.6%; Palhagen2001 rufinamide up to 1600 mg/day 23%)","rationale":"Listed differentials match PubMed abstracts: Biton 32.5% vs 14.3% = 18.2%; Brodie 28.2% vs 18.6% = 9.6%; Palhagen 39% vs 16% = 23%.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/"]},{"current_value":"13.45-93 % (drug minus placebo MPC differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Ohtsuka2014 rufinamide adjunctive LGS target/max regimen 20.9%; Biton2010 rufinamide 3200 mg/day 13.45%; Brodie2009 rufinamide 3200 mg/day 22%; Glauser2008 rufinamide adjunctive LGS target/max regimen 21%; Palhagen2001 rufinamide up to 1600 mg/day 93%)","field":"diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"13.45-93 % (drug minus placebo MPC differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Ohtsuka2014 rufinamide adjunctive LGS target/max regimen 20.9%; Biton2010 rufinamide 3200 mg/day 13.45%; Brodie2009 rufinamide 3200 mg/day 22%; Glauser2008 rufinamide adjunctive LGS target/max regimen 21%; Palhagen2001 rufinamide up to 1600 mg/day 93%)","rationale":"All listed median-percent-change differentials match the cited abstracts when seizure-frequency increases are treated as negative reductions: Ohtsuka 24.2-3.3=20.9; Biton 23.25-9.80=13.45; Brodie 20.4-(-1.6)=22.0; Glauser 32.7-11.7=21.0; Palhagen 41-(-52)=93.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25219353/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"The cited primary PubMed abstracts do not provide a drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential suitable for this row.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25219353/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20061123/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/"]},{"current_value":"Biton2010|18.2|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/|357; Brodie2009|9.6|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/|313; Palhagen2001|23|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/|50","field":"plot_diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Biton2010|18.2|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/|357; Brodie2009|9.6|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/|313; Palhagen2001|23|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/|50","rationale":"Plot entries match cited PubMed abstracts and randomized sample sizes.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/"]},{"current_value":"Ohtsuka2014|20.9|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25219353/|59; Biton2010|13.45|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/|357; Brodie2009|22|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/|313; Glauser2008|21|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/|138; Palhagen2001|93|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/|50","field":"plot_diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Ohtsuka2014|20.9|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25219353/|59; Biton2010|13.45|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/|357; Brodie2009|22|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/|313; Glauser2008|21|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/|138; Palhagen2001|93|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/|50","rationale":"Plot entries match cited PubMed abstracts and randomized sample sizes.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25219353/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No extractable seizure-freedom differential was verified from the cited primary PubMed abstracts.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25219353/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20061123/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/"]}],"overall_status":"needs_update","proposed_row_updates":[{"current_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current DailyMed label.","field":"fda_black_box_warning","proposed_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current FDA/openFDA label.","reason":"DailyMed is not permitted for boxed-warning verification; openFDA/FDA source should be named.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d%22&limit=5"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=no_boxed_warning_in_selected_label; setid=0a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d; published=Nov 18, 2024; title=BANZEL (RUFINAMIDE) TABLET, FILM COATED BANZEL (RUFINAMIDE) SUSPENSION [EISAI INC.]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=0a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA drug label API; status=no_boxed_warning_in_selected_fda_label; spl_set_id=0a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d; effective_time=20221215; title=Banzel / RUFINAMIDE; api_url=https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d%22&limit=5","reason":"Replace non-permitted DailyMed boxed-warning source with FDA/openFDA metadata.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d%22&limit=5"]},{"current_value":"CNS: somnolence 24%, GI: vomiting 22%, CNS: headache 16%, CNS: dizziness 14%, GI: nausea 12%, dermatologic: rash 5%, cardiac: QT shortening 14%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: somnolence 24.3% in LGS RCT; GI: vomiting 21.6% in LGS RCT; CNS: headache 16% pediatric pooled / 27% adult pooled; CNS: dizziness 8% pediatric pooled / 19% adult pooled; GI: nausea 7% pediatric pooled / 12% adult pooled; dermatologic: rash 4% pediatric pooled; cardiac: QT shortening >20 msec at Tmax in 46% at 3200 mg vs 5-10% placebo in QT study.","reason":"Correct unsupported dizziness, rash, and QT-shortening percentages while preserving supported somnolence/vomiting values.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/","https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Inhibitor","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"Inducer","reason":"FDA label supports weak CYP3A4 induction, not a clinically meaningful inhibitor classification.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"Renal/no major metabolism","field":"filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Carboxylesterase hydrolysis/no major CYP metabolism","reason":"FDA label states most elimination is via carboxylesterase-mediated metabolism; unchanged renal excretion is less than 2%.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"400 mg/day adult starting dose; titrate to 3200 mg/day target/max","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Adults: start 400-800 mg/day in two divided doses and titrate by 400-800 mg every other day to 3200 mg/day maximum; Elger2010 dose-ranging partial-seizure study reported a minimally efficacious dose of 400 mg/day.","reason":"Clarifies that 400 mg/day is FDA starting-dose language for adults and the minimum-efficacious-dose claim comes from a partial-seizure dose-ranging RCT.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20061123/"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA Banzel prescribing information","reason":"Use the FDA label directly as the trusted source.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA Banzel prescribing information; FDA/openFDA drug label API; FDA Orphan Drug Designations and Approvals; NCBI Bookshelf LiverTox rufinamide monograph; NCBI Bookshelf StatPearls rufinamide; EMA Inovelon EPAR; PubMed RCT records.","reason":"Replace unsupported/non-trusted source labels with sources that actually support row facts.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=193504","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548457/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557595/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/inovelon"]}],"rct_link_checks":[{"label":"Ohtsuka2014","pmid":"25219353","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"PubMed identifies rufinamide adjunctive therapy for LGS in a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial; abstract reports 59 randomized patients and placebo comparator.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25219353/"},{"label":"Elger2010","pmid":"20061123","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"PubMed identifies a 24-week multicenter Phase II randomized, double-blind, parallel-group, five-arm study including placebo and rufinamide 200-1600 mg/day.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20061123/"},{"label":"Biton2010","pmid":"20887365","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"PubMed identifies adjunctive rufinamide 3200 mg/day for refractory partial-onset seizures in a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group multicenter trial with 357 randomized patients.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20887365/"},{"label":"Brodie2009","pmid":"19490053","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"PubMed identifies adjunctive rufinamide 1600 mg BID/3200 mg/day in a double-blind, placebo-controlled, randomized, parallel-group multicenter trial with 313 randomized subjects.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19490053/"},{"label":"Glauser2008","pmid":"18401024","proposed_action":"retain; do not append the deterministic warning that the title lacks randomized/placebo language","rationale":"Although the title does not contain the design words, the PubMed abstract explicitly states a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled rufinamide trial in LGS and reports 138 treated patients. The update-check concern is a false positive.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18401024/"},{"label":"Palhagen2001","pmid":"11164700","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"PubMed identifies a 28-day double-blind, placebo-controlled proof-of-principle trial of adjunctive rufinamide 400-1600 mg/day in 50 patients with epilepsy, with randomized rufinamide and placebo groups.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11164700/"}],"source_updates":[{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA Banzel prescribing information; FDA/openFDA drug label API; FDA Orphan Drug Designations and Approvals; NCBI Bookshelf LiverTox rufinamide monograph; NCBI Bookshelf StatPearls rufinamide; EMA Inovelon EPAR; PubMed RCT records.","reason":"Current source list includes non-trusted or non-specific sources and does not reliably support each row fact.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=193504","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548457/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557595/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/inovelon"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=no_boxed_warning_in_selected_label; setid=0a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d; published=Nov 18, 2024; title=BANZEL (RUFINAMIDE) TABLET, FILM COATED BANZEL (RUFINAMIDE) SUSPENSION [EISAI INC.]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=0a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA drug label API; status=no_boxed_warning_in_selected_fda_label; spl_set_id=0a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d; effective_time=20221215; title=Banzel / RUFINAMIDE; api_url=https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d%22&limit=5","reason":"Black-box warning verification must use FDA/openFDA, FDA labels, or Drugs@FDA, not DailyMed.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220a3fa925-1abd-458a-bd57-4ae780a1ef2d%22&limit=5"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA Banzel prescribing information","reason":"The mechanism text is supported by FDA label lines describing sodium-channel modulation; DailyMed does not need to be cited.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.fda.gov/media/169593/download"]}],"summary":"Rufinamide/Banzel/Inovelon is the same drug, and the core indication, formulation, dose ceiling, half-life, mechanism, QT-shortening warning, U.S. approval year, and six listed PubMed RCT links are supported. The row needs updates for source policy and several cell facts: boxed-warning source must be FDA/openFDA instead of DailyMed, adverse-event percentages include unsupported/incorrect dizziness, rash, and QT values, filter_enzyme_effect should be Inducer, filter_metabolism should reflect carboxylesterase hydrolysis/no major CYP metabolism, and evidence_sources should be replaced with trusted sources that actually support the retained facts."}
